closures: Func literals and captures

A func literal that is only called where it is made (func() {...}() or f := func() {...}
with every use of f a call, not go, not defer, not stored, passed or returned, and no
recover inside) cannot outlive the call: it is a plain C++ lambda that captures by
reference, with no gp::func and no allocation, and a local variable that only such closures
capture is not boxed (ADR-114; a parameter or a result that one captures and changes still
is).

A literal that go or defer runs directly is also the lambda itself, handed to gp::go_call or
gp_push_call, with its captures copied into it.

Any other func literal, used as a value, is a C++ lambda wrapped in gp::func<R(A...)>. The
lambda has a hidden template parameter, <int = N>, which numbers the literal for the stack
trace (main.f.func1, as Go names it). Copies of a gp::func share the closure's state.

A captured variable is copied into the lambda when that is safe: the closure never sees a
change, because the variable never changes after it is declared, and it is at most 128
bytes. Otherwise the variable is boxed (see gocpp explain boxing) and the lambda captures
the box, so the function and the closure share one variable, as in Go. The annotation says
which of the two happened for each captured name.

A closure that refers to itself (a recursive func literal stored in a variable it captures)
makes a reference cycle: the reference counts never reach zero, and the memory is not freed.
gocpp vet, once it is built (Phase 13), is planned to report the obvious cases.

Specification: docs/spec/cpp-mapping.md#closures
See also: gocpp explain boxing, gocpp explain defer
Also known as: closure, capture, lambda, funclit

Example (closures.gp):

    package main

    import "fmt"

    func counter() func() int {
    	n := 0 // captured and changed: shared between the function and the closure
    	return func() int {
    		n++
    		return n
    	}
    }

    func main() {
    	step := 10 // captured by a closure that is called in place: stays on the stack
    	add := func(x int) int { return x + step }
    	next := counter()
    	fmt.Println(add(1), next(), next())

    	var fs []func() int
    	for i := 0; i < 2; i++ { // each iteration has its own i; the closures keep them
    		fs = append(fs, func() int { return i })
    	}
    	fmt.Println(fs[0](), fs[1]())
    }

The C++ that gocpp emit -annotate writes for it:

    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #pragma once
    #include <gocpp/rt.hpp>

    namespace gpkg_main {
    gp::func<int64_t()> counter();
    void main();

    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/closures.gp.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/closures.gp.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    namespace gpkg_main {

    // ---- ./closures.gp:5: func counter() func() int {
    gp::func<int64_t()> counter() {

    // ---- ./closures.gp:6: n := 0 // captured and changed: shared between the function and the closure
    // gp[boxing] col 2: n is boxed: a closure captures it and it changes, so the function and the
    //     closure share one box (a copy would miss the changes)
        gp::ptr<int64_t> n = gp::new_<int64_t>(0);

    // ---- ./closures.gp:7: return func() int {
    // gp[closures] col 9: func literal #1 is a C++ lambda (the stack trace names it func1); what it
    //     captures is copied into the lambda (a box is shared), and a lambda that is used as a func
    //     value is wrapped in gp::func, whose copies share the captured state
        return gp::func<int64_t()>([n]<int = 1>() mutable -> int64_t {

    // ---- ./closures.gp:8: n++
    // gp[closures] col 3: captures n by sharing its box (it changes after capture)
            (*n)++;

    // ---- ./closures.gp:9: return n
            return (*n);

    // ---- ./closures.gp:10: }
        }

    // ---- ./closures.gp:7: return func() int {  (continued)
        );

    // ---- ./closures.gp:11: }
    }

    // ---- ./closures.gp:13: func main() {
    void main() {

    // ---- ./closures.gp:14: step := 10 // captured by a closure that is called in place: stays on the stack
        int64_t step = 10;

    // ---- ./closures.gp:15: add := func(x int) int { return x + step }
    // gp[closures] col 9: func literal #1 is a local closure: it is only called where it is made, so it
    //     is a plain C++ lambda (no gp::func, no allocation) that captures by reference
    // gp[closures] col 38: captures step by reference: it stays on the C++ stack and the closure runs
    //     within the function
        auto add = [&step]<int = 1>(int64_t x) mutable -> int64_t { return x + step; };

    // ---- ./closures.gp:16: next := counter()
        gp::func<int64_t()> next = counter();

    // ---- ./closures.gp:17: fmt.Println(add(1), next(), next())
    // gp[interfaces] col 2: fmt.Println: the runtime's gp::fmt functions take each operand with the
    //     descriptor of its Go type (gp::fmt::as), not boxed into an interface first (an operand of a
    //     non-pointer type with an Error method is, see the allocation note)
    // gp[checks] col 14: call of a local closure or method expression: a direct call of the lambda;
    //     there is no func value, so no nil check
    // gp[order] col 14: gp_t0 holds the result of a call (or a value that a later call could change):
    //     Go evaluates it first, in order, where C++ leaves the order of the operands unspecified
    // gp[checks] col 22: call of a func value: gp::func's call operator checks for nil and panics like
    //     Go (gocpp build -nochecks removes it)
    // gp[order] col 22: gp_t1 holds the result of a call (or a value that a later call could change):
    //     Go evaluates it first, in order, where C++ leaves the order of the operands unspecified
    // gp[checks] col 30: call of a func value: gp::func's call operator checks for nil and panics like
    //     Go (gocpp build -nochecks removes it)
        { auto gp_t0 = add(1); auto gp_t1 = next(); gp::fmt::Println(gp_t0, gp_t1, next()); }

    // ---- ./closures.gp:19: var fs []func() int
        gp::slice<gp::func<int64_t()>> fs{};

    // ---- ./closures.gp:20: for i := 0; i < 2; i++ { // each iteration has its own i; the closures keep them
    // gp[allocation] col 2: each iteration has its own i (Go 1.22) and it is boxed (see its boxing
    //     note): before the post statement the loop copies it into a new box, one allocation per
    //     iteration; a loop variable that nothing captures stays a plain variable
    // gp[boxing] col 6: i is boxed: a closure captures it and it changes, so the function and the
    //     closure share one box (a copy would miss the changes)
        for (gp::ptr<int64_t> i = gp::new_<int64_t>(0); (*i) < 2;) {
            {

    // ---- ./closures.gp:21: fs = append(fs, func() int { return i })
    // gp[allocation] col 8: append: writes in place when the capacity is enough, else allocates a
    //     bigger array (the capacity is Go's: its growth formula, element sizes and malloc size
    //     classes); the result may share the array with the argument
    // gp[closures] col 19: func literal #2 is a C++ lambda (the stack trace names it func2); what it
    //     captures is copied into the lambda (a box is shared), and a lambda that is used as a func
    //     value is wrapped in gp::func, whose copies share the captured state
    // gp[closures] col 39: captures i by sharing its box (it changes after capture)
                fs = gp::append(static_cast<gp::slice<gp::func<int64_t()>>&&>(fs), gp::func<int64_t()>([i]<int = 2>() mutable -> int64_t { return (*i); }));

    // ---- ./closures.gp:22: }
            }

    // ---- ./closures.gp:20: for i := 0; i < 2; i++ { // each iteration has its own i; the closures keep them  (continued)
            i = gp::new_<int64_t>(*i);
            (*i)++;

    // ---- ./closures.gp:22: }  (continued)
        }

    // ---- ./closures.gp:23: fmt.Println(fs[0](), fs[1]())
    // gp[checks] col 14: call of a func value that the callee could change (a boxed or shared
    //     variable): gp_call keeps a copy of it alive during the call; a nil func panics like Go (gocpp
    //     build -nochecks removes it)
    // gp[order] col 14: gp_t2 holds the result of a call (or a value that a later call could change):
    //     Go evaluates it first, in order, where C++ leaves the order of the operands unspecified
    // gp[checks] col 16: s[i] of a slice: gp::slice::operator[] checks the index and panics with Go's
    //     "index out of range" message (gocpp build -nochecks removes it)
    // gp[checks] col 23: call of a func value that the callee could change (a boxed or shared
    //     variable): gp_call keeps a copy of it alive during the call; a nil func panics like Go (gocpp
    //     build -nochecks removes it)
    // gp[checks] col 25: s[i] of a slice: gp::slice::operator[] checks the index and panics with Go's
    //     "index out of range" message (gocpp build -nochecks removes it)
        { auto gp_t2 = fs[0].gp_call(); gp::fmt::Println(gp_t2, fs[1].gp_call()); }

    // ---- ./closures.gp:24: }
    }

    } // namespace gpkg_main
